#original-page(612pt, 792pt, (image("graphics/page-01.png", width: 231.5pt), 317.5pt, 525.5pt))[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(145.94pt, 81.38pt, "Geant4 Modeling of Energy and Charge Deposition in Satellites Solar Cells ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(210.53pt, 104.3pt, "Youssef Abouhussien*, Gennady Miloshevsky")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.48pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(398.47pt, 100.82pt, "†")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(99.384pt, 127.34pt, "*")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(104.3pt, 127.34pt, "Virginia Commonwealth University, 401 West Main St, Richmond, VA 23284, abouhussieny@vcu.edu ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.48pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(110.42pt, 135.38pt, "†")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(113.66pt, 138.86pt, "Virginia Commonwealth University, 401 West Main St, Richmond, VA 23284, gennady@vcu.edu ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(54pt, 173.3pt, "ABSTRACT ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(54pt, 196.34pt, "Understanding radiation effects in spacecraft components is ")
  #line-at(54pt, 207.86pt, "critical for predicting long-term performance degradation. In ")
  #line-at(54pt, 219.38pt, "this work, a Geant4 Monte Carlo model is developed to ")
  #line-at(54pt, 230.9pt, "compute charge and energy deposition in satellite solar-cell ")
  #line-at(54pt, 242.3pt, "materials exposed to electrons, protons, and X-ray ")
  #line-at(54pt, 253.82pt, "environments. The model is validated against published ")
  #line-at(54pt, 265.37pt, "experimental and computational benchmarks and shows ")
  #line-at(54pt, 276.89pt, "strong agreement across multiple energy ranges. Power-")
  #line-at(54pt, 288.41pt, "density deposition profiles are then evaluated for a multi-")
  #line-at(54pt, 299.93pt, "layer solar-cell structure under blackbody soft X-rays, ")
  #line-at(54pt, 311.33pt, "mono-energetic X-rays, and high-energy electrons. The ")
  #line-at(54pt, 322.85pt, "results show that low-energy X-rays dominate damage in ")
  #line-at(54pt, 334.37pt, "surface layers, while high-energy particles penetrate deeper ")
  #line-at(54pt, 345.89pt, "into semiconductor and substrate layers, posing risk to ")
  #line-at(54pt, 357.41pt, "electronic components. These findings highlight the ")
  #line-at(54pt, 368.81pt, "importance of radiation-specific shielding strategies for ")
  #line-at(54pt, 380.33pt, "space solar-cell design. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(54pt, 403.37pt, "INTRODUCTION ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 426.41pt, "Modeling of radiation damage in spacecraft and satellite ")
  #line-at(54pt, 437.83pt, "material is very important to scientists who are doing research ")
  #line-at(54pt, 449.35pt, "on materials degradation analysis. Satellites in the Earth’s ")
  #line-at(54pt, 460.87pt, "orbit are subjected mainly to high-energy electrons trapped in ")
  #line-at(54pt, 472.39pt, "Earth’s magnetic field (the Van Allen belts), high-energy ")
  #line-at(54pt, 483.91pt, "protons from solar flares, and high-energy particles from ")
  #line-at(54pt, 495.43pt, "cosmic rays [1]. There’s also a potential hazard from nuclear ")
  #line-at(54pt, 506.83pt, "detonation and electromagnetic pulse attack, which would ")
  #line-at(54pt, 518.35pt, "make satellites in Earth’s orbit vulnerable and at high risk of ")
  #line-at(54pt, 529.87pt, "damage [1], [2]. These kinds of radiation affect satellite ")
  #line-at(54pt, 541.39pt, "surface materials in many ways, mainly due to charge and ")
  #line-at(54pt, 552.91pt, "energy deposition, which can cause great damage to the ")
  #line-at(54pt, 564.31pt, "surface material of the spacecraft. Charge deposition can ")
  #line-at(54pt, 575.83pt, "accumulate inside insulators and cause electrostatic ")
  #line-at(54pt, 587.35pt, "discharge, while energy deposition can cause mechanical ")
  #line-at(54pt, 598.87pt, "failure [3]. Other studies has calculated the energy deposition ")
  #line-at(54pt, 610.42pt, "effects from X-rays irradiation in the surface materials of ")
  #line-at(54pt, 621.94pt, "satellites [4], [5]. ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 633.34pt, "In this paper, we reported our results from a Geant4 code ")
  #line-at(54pt, 644.86pt, "that calculates the charge and energy deposition in satellite ")
  #line-at(54pt, 656.38pt, "surface materials. We validated our code against multiple ")
  #line-at(54pt, 667.9pt, "experimental and other computational data for electrons, ")
  #line-at(54pt, 679.42pt, "protons, and X-rays. Then we calculated power density ")
  #line-at(54pt, 690.94pt, "distribution in a solar cell for blackbody X-ray spectra at ")
  #line-at(54pt, 702.336pt, "temperature of 1 keV and compared it against both high ")
  #line-at(54pt, 713.856pt, "energy electrons and mono-energetic X-ray beams. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(315.05pt, 173.3pt, "Geant4 Model and Validation ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(333.07pt, 196.34pt, "The energy deposition and charge deposited by particles ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 207.86pt, "in materials can be evaluated using the Monte Carlo (MC) ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 219.38pt, "approach. Geant4 provides a complete set of tools using MC ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 230.9pt, "method for all areas of radiation transport simulation, ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 242.3pt, "geometry tracking, and material response [6], [7]. The low-")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 253.82pt, "energy Livermore model is used to describe the ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 265.37pt, "electromagnetic physics processes for photons and electrons ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 276.89pt, "including photo-absorption, Rayleigh scattering, Compton ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 288.41pt, "scattering, gamma conversion, impact ionization, X-ray ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 299.93pt, "fluorescence, Auger electrons, Auger cascade, as well as ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 311.33pt, "generation of secondary particles [8]. Different production ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 322.85pt, "cutoffs has been used for validation (1 µm to 1 nm).  ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 334.37pt, "Validations against experimental and other computational ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 345.89pt, "data are illustrated in Fig. 1 through Fig. 5. Aluminum and ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 357.41pt, "ammonium perchlorate are widely used in aerospace industry ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 368.81pt, "especially in solid propulsion for rockets [9]. The purpose is ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 380.33pt, "to validate the code for high energy electrons for both charge ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 391.85pt, "and energy deposition, charge deposition by protons as well ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 403.37pt, "as energy deposition by soft X-rays, which deposit most of ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 414.89pt, "their energy at the surface of materials [2] ")
  #line-at(333.07pt, 426.41pt, "The shapes of the curves from Geant4 simulation and the ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 437.83pt, "data from experimental and computational results are found ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 449.35pt, "to be in very good agreement for charge distribution of ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 460.87pt, "electrons (Fig. 1 and Fig. 2), although there’s a slight shift in ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 472.39pt, "the peak (Fig. 1), which might be due to experimental ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 483.91pt, "uncertainty as the data is relatively old. Electron energies of ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 495.43pt, "300 keV and 1 MeV has been chosen to validate the code in ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 506.83pt, "low and high energy ranges for different materials. ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 684.1pt, "Fig. 1. Probability distribution of 300 keV electrons in ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 695.496pt, "aluminum compared against the computational data [9]. ")
]

// arXiv source page 2
#original-page(612pt, 792pt, (image("graphics/page-02.png", width: 501.5pt), 55.5pt, 75pt))[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(54pt, 251.9pt, "Fig. 2. Charge density rate of 1 MeV electrons in ammonium ")
  #line-at(54pt, 263.33pt, "perchlorate against results from NASA report [9].  ")
  #line-at(54pt, 286.37pt, "Electron charge deposition is low near the surface as ")
  #line-at(54pt, 297.89pt, "secondary generated electrons tend to escape, which can be ")
  #line-at(54pt, 309.41pt, "seen clearly in Fig. 2 as positive charge appears on the ")
  #line-at(54pt, 320.93pt, "surface. ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 332.33pt, "In Figs. 3 and 4, the code has been validated for energy ")
  #line-at(54pt, 343.85pt, "deposition in single and multi-slab system. The energy ")
  #line-at(54pt, 355.37pt, "deposition curves agree well with the experimental data aside ")
  #line-at(54pt, 366.89pt, "from the peaks near the interfaces (Fig. 4), which can be ")
  #line-at(54pt, 378.41pt, "attributed to the uncertainty of the data near the interface ")
  #line-at(54pt, 389.93pt, "boundary, and since the deposition at the interface boundary ")
  #line-at(54pt, 401.33pt, "is considered to belong to the material on the left-side. The ")
  #line-at(54pt, 412.85pt, "units of the y-axis makes the difference very noticeable as the ")
  #line-at(54pt, 424.37pt, "energy is divided by the material’s density [10]. ")
  #line-at(54pt, 587.95pt, "Fig. 3. Energy deposition of 1 MeV electrons in aluminum ")
  #line-at(54pt, 599.35pt, "compared against the experimental data [10] ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 232.34pt, "Fig. 4. Energy deposition of 1 MeV electrons in Be-Au-Be ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 243.86pt, "slab system [10]. ")
  #line-at(333.07pt, 266.93pt, "The ability of code to capture positive charge distribution ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 278.33pt, "from protons is shown in Fig. 5, the semi-empirical data is for ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 289.85pt, "proton’s mean range in aluminum, while Geant4 simulation ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 301.37pt, "result is for charge distribution. The peak agrees well with the ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 312.89pt, "proton range in the material, which should correspond to a ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 324.41pt, "positive charge at this mean range. As it’s shown in Fig. 5, ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 335.93pt, "protons locally deposit all their energy upon first collision ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 347.33pt, "within the material and stop there, unlike electrons which ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 358.85pt, "deposit their energy in a continuous manner [11]. ")
  #line-at(333.07pt, 370.37pt, "In Fig. 6, the dose profile agrees well with the ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 381.89pt, "computational results from DTRA report using a blackbody ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 393.41pt, "X-ray spectrum with temperature of 1 keV (Fig. 7). This ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 404.93pt, "energy range is particularly of interest as the soft X-rays ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 416.33pt, "damage the surface of the spacecraft and doesn’t penetrate ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 427.87pt, "deep.  ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 615.94pt, "Fig. 5. Charge density rate of 6 MeV protons in aluminum ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 627.46pt, "compared against the experimental result for mean proton ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 638.86pt, "range [11]. ")
]

// arXiv source page 3
#original-page(612pt, 792pt, (image("graphics/page-03.png", width: 510pt), 54pt, 74pt))[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(54pt, 232.46pt, "Fig. 6. Normalized dose in poco-graphite for blackbody X-")
  #line-at(54pt, 243.98pt, "ray spectrum at T = 1 keV compared against the ")
  #line-at(54pt, 255.53pt, "computational results taken from DTRA report [2] ")
  #line-at(54pt, 423.53pt, "Fig. 7. Black-body spectrum of X-rays at T= 1 keV [2]. ")
  #line-at(54pt, 446.47pt, "In contrast, high energy X-rays penetrate deep into the ")
  #line-at(54pt, 457.99pt, "material where they deposit most of energy and inflict more ")
  #line-at(54pt, 469.51pt, "damage to the electronic components [2]. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(54pt, 515.47pt, "RESULTS ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 538.51pt, "We calculated power density profiles in a multi-layer ")
  #line-at(54pt, 550.03pt, "system of a satellite solar cell with the configuration of layers ")
  #line-at(54pt, 561.55pt, "given in TABLE I. The simulation was performed for ")
  #line-at(54pt, 572.95pt, "different X-ray energies and 1 MeV electrons as shown in ")
  #line-at(54pt, 584.47pt, "Fig. 8. We compared the energy deposition distribution for ")
  #line-at(54pt, 595.99pt, "different radiation environments that the satellite might be ")
  #line-at(54pt, 607.54pt, "vulnerable to. As it’s shown in Fig. 8, in the case of X-rays, ")
  #line-at(54pt, 619.06pt, "the outermost 2 layers experience the most power density ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 81.38pt, "deposition from a black body spectrum of T = 1 keV (Fig. 6) ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 92.9pt, "and then it keeps decreasing as the X-rays don’t penetrate ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 104.3pt, "much deeper beyond the last Kapton layer. We can see a ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 115.82pt, "similar behavior for the mono-energetic 1 keV case, but with ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 127.34pt, "higher value. In contrast, for the 1 MeV mono-energetic hard ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 138.86pt, "X-rays the power density deposition is much less than the ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 150.38pt, "former two cases in the first 2 layers, and starts increasing in ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 161.9pt, "the fifth layer. It keeps penetrating deeper beyond the solar-")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 173.3pt, "cell layers. In case of 1 MeV electrons, it appears that ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 184.82pt, "electrons ")
  #line-at(364.073pt, 184.82pt, "continuously ")
  #line-at(428.684pt, 184.82pt, "deposit ")
  #line-at(470.62pt, 184.82pt, "power ")
  #line-at(508.617pt, 184.82pt, "density ")
  #line-at(550.34pt, 184.82pt, "in ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 196.34pt, "approximately homogenous manner in the first 6 layers. It ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 207.86pt, "continues to deposit deeper beyond the solar cell.  ")
  #line-at(333.07pt, 219.38pt, "In conclusion, low energy X-rays (1 - 10 keV) induce ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 230.9pt, "more damage to the solar-cell than high energy X-rays in ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 242.3pt, "terms of power density in the outermost layers. High energy ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 253.82pt, "electrons deposit a higher overall power density in the whole ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 265.37pt, "solar cell. We can also conclude from the curve in Fig. 8. that ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 276.89pt, "the 1 MeV X-rays would deposit most of its energy beyond ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 288.41pt, "the solar cell. Therefore, it deposits more energy in the ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 299.93pt, "electronic components than low energy X-rays and 1 MeV ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 311.33pt, "electrons. It should also be noted that energy deposition isn’t ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 322.85pt, "the only damaging mechanism in case of electrons. Electrons ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 334.37pt, "can deposit its charge inside the solar cell, which increases ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 345.89pt, "the risk of local electrostatic discharge in the dielectric ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 357.41pt, "materials. Further studies would be needed to investigate the ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 368.81pt, "overall effect of high energy particles on the components ")
  #line-at(315.05pt, 380.33pt, "beyond the solar cell. ")
  #line-at(325.85pt, 403.37pt, "TABLE I. Solar cell layers ")
  #line-at(325.85pt, 419.09pt, "Layers (counted from left in Fig. 8)     Thickness, μm ")
  #line-at(325.85pt, 439.63pt, "1")
